ot going to happen  anytime soon I think most people would  agree whether you like  or not that's a debate but it's not  happening anytime soon so the the  immediate thing that we should look at  doing is what is actually doable which  is socially democratic programs that  will seriously deal with homelessness  and the rising cost of living across the  United States Bernie Sanders has now  released such a proposal and it's a very  solid one and I want to talk about it  with you today the premise is that we  have a situation where it's frankly  outrageous that we have homelessness in  the United States we're a rich country  now we have the money financially we can  end homelessness in the United States we  should consider homes a place to live a  right cost of living is out of control  rent seeking and gentrification at the  hands of wealthy developers and  landowners and rich people it hurts so  many people and Bernie Sanders plan  deals with a lot of this now I'm just  gonna give you some of the highlights I  know some people will say I missed some  important elements of this there are  more than 30 provisions but I want to  focus on some of the important ones from  Bernie's housing for all proposal there  will be one nearly one and a half  trillion dollars spent over 10 years to  build and renovate nearly  seven-and-a-half million affordable  housing units in mixed income  developments as well as four hundred  billion dollars for two million mixed  income social housing units for  desegregation and integration Boston  actually does some of this where  affordable housing units are placed the  you might have a 12 unit development and  a certain number of those are designated  affordable which helps to integrate  rather than segregate people on the  basis of income it's not perfect but it  does do that to a great degree Bernie  would expand USDA section five one five  by five hundred million dollars to build  affordable units in rural areas as well  as protect affordable units existing  affordable units very important you know  we talked about how can Democrats appeal  to rural voters I'm going to deal with  this more on tomorrow's show as well  this is a great idea to go along those  lines Indian housing block grants will  be increased to three billion dollars in  total funding there will be seventy  billion dollars allocated to repair and  update public housing including  high-speed broadband which is in  port and we've talked about broadband as  a having it versus not having it is is  increasingly a segregating factor  economically the Faircloth amendment  will be repealed that amendment prevents  building new public housing in certain  situations via GND we were the green New  Deal that is there will be the  electrification and decarbonisation of  public housing which is both good for  the climate and also good for the people  who are in public housing a section 8  rental assistance will be fully funded  at the rate of four hundred and ten  billion dollars over ten years makes  sense there will be a strengthening of  the Fair Housing Act and the addition of  a non-discrimination law this small area  fair market rent rule will be expanded  and there will be two billion dollars  via the USDA combined with six billion  dollars via HUD for a first-time  homebuyer program first-time homebuyer  programs have existed for a long time  but the idea is let's increase it let's  strengthen it in order to deal with the  high cost of living that still keeps  people who are just a little bit too  wealthy to qualify for some of these  programs from buying we can enable them  to become first-time homebuyers as well  and then end the mass sale of mortgages  to Wall Street this is something a lot  of people don't even know happens but  very often there are lots of banks who  you will take out a mortgage with them  and very soon thereafter they will sell  the mortgage to a big Wall Street firm  this is an extraordinarily speculative  activity 